 Let's start by taking the common denominator $(x-1)(x-7)$, and rewrite the inequality. \\ We have \begin{aligned} & \frac{1}{x-1}-\frac{1}{x-7}>1 \\ \Leftarrow & \frac{x-7}{(x-1)(x-7)}-\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x-7)}>1\\ \Leftarrow & \frac{((x-7)-(x-1))}{(x-1)(x-7)}>1 \\ \Leftarrow &\frac{-6}{(x-1)(x-7)}>1}\end{aligned} Now remember an inequality like $x>1$ is equivalent to $x^2-1>0$, or $(x-1)(x+1)>0$. This gives us two ranges for $x-7$ and $x-1$: \begin{aligned}&(x-7)(x-1)<0,\\ \Rightarrow &x-1<0\;\text{or}\;x-7>0\\ \Rightarrow &x<1\;\text{or}\;x>7\\ &x-7>0\;\text{and}\;\\ &x-1<0\;\text{or}x-7>-x+1\\ \Rightarrow &x>7\;\text{and}\;x>1 \end{aligned} Therefore, we uwould have X<1
User: Why can we not simply say that $x-1$ and $x-7$ is some value between $0$ and $1$ when $x$ is somewhere between $1$ and $7$, making the ratio go from some value from, say, $0.3$ to $0.9$ when $x$ is some value, $m$ (e.g. $0.2$). Wouldn't it? We could have not even checked the inequality right?
1. If $x<1$, then
\begin{align}
\frac1{x-1}-\frac1{x-7}=-(\frac1{x-1}-\frac1{x-7})
\end{align}
Which is bigger than $1$.
2. If $1<x<7$, then
\begin{align}
\frac1{x-1}-\frac1{x-7}=\frac{(x-7)-(x-1)}{(x-1)(x-7)}
\end{align}
To check if this is bigger than $1$, we need $\frac{(x-7)-(x-1)(x-1)(x-7))}{(x-1)(x-7)}>\frac{(x-1)(x-7)}{(x-1)(x-7)}$.
That is, we need$$(x-7)-(x-1)>1\qquad\Leftrightarrow\qquad -x>-5\Leftrightarrow\qquad x<5$$
In conclusion $x$ need be greater than $-5$ and less than $7$ in order for $\frac1{x-1}-\frac1{x-7}$ to be bigger than $1$.

